Confirmed: nested ThemeProvider renders `<div class="g-root g-root_theme_<theme>">` around children (scoped because it has a parent provider). So I can control theme inside App without touching main.tsx. The scoped div needs to fill the viewport and set `background: var(--g-color-base-background)`.

Now write the code.

**Design:**

`src/setup.ts`:
```ts
import {configure, Lang} from '@gravity-ui/uikit';
import {DashKit} from '@gravity-ui/dashkit';
import {pluginTitle, pluginText} from '@gravity-ui/dashkit';
import {chartPlugin} from './plugins/chart';
import {metricPlugin} from './plugins/metric';

export function setupDashboard() { ... }
```
Must run once before DashKit renders. Use module-level execution (import side effect) or a guard boolean.

Register plugins: pluginTitle, pluginText (plain text, no apiHandler → renders plain text; withMarkdown false when no apiHandler? Let me check `get withMarkdown` at line 103 of Text.js). It said `withMarkdown` getter — check quickly. If withMarkdown depends on apiHandler presence, pluginText without settings renders plain text in a div. Fine.

`src/plugins/chart.tsx`:
```tsx
import {Chart} from '@gravity-ui/charts';
import type {ChartData} from '@gravity-ui/charts';
import type {Plugin, PluginWidgetProps} from '@gravity-ui/dashkit';

type ChartWidgetData = {title: string; chart: ChartData};

function ChartWidget(props: PluginWidgetProps) {
  const data = props.data as unknown as ChartWidgetData;
  return (
    <WidgetFrame title={data.title}>
      <Chart data={data.chart} />
    </WidgetFrame>
  );
}

export const chartPlugin: Plugin = {
  type: 'chart',
  defaultLayout: {w: 12, h: 12, minH: 6, minW: 6},
  renderer(props, forwardedRef) {
    return <ChartWidget {...props} ref={forwardedRef} />;  // hmm — renderer returns ReactNode; forwardedRef expects instance
  },
};
```
Wait — renderer signature: `(props, forwardedRef) => React.ReactNode`. If my component is a function component, `ref` won't work unless forwardRef. The built-in plugins pass ref to class components. For function components, just don't pass the ref (it's optional/undefined-able). Simply `renderer: (props) => <ChartWidget {...props} />`. But do they require ref forwarding for adjustWidgetLayout? Only for auto-height plugins. Not needed for charts. But to be safe, I can attach the ref to a div via callback: `renderer: (props, ref) => <div ref={ref as any}>...`? PluginRef is `object | null`. The built-in title plugin passes ref to class component. For safety, wrap in forwardRef and attach to root div.

Chart sizing: `Chart` fills its parent; parent needs explicit height. The widget content area: GridItem renders Item which sizes the plugin container? Let me check how Item sizes the plugin root. In DataLens custom plugins typically use `adjustWidgetLayout` or rely on the item wrapper having 100% height. GridItem css: `.dashkit-grid-item` has height from RGL. Item renders plugin inside. Let me check Item.js to see if the plugin wrapper gets height:100%.

Actually `PluginWidgetProps` includes `width` and `height` (optional) — hmm, those may be passed when adjustWidgetLayout used. Simplest robust approach: CSS — make `.dashkit-grid-item > div` chain 100%. Let me inspect Item.js / GridItem.js DOM structure quickly.

