events.content:
`.dashkit-item__renderer` is `position:absolute; inset:0` — the plugin renderer root gets the full item area. So my plugin root div will fill the widget if it's the direct child with height:100%. The renderer output is placed inside `.dashkit-item__renderer`, so my root needs `height: 100%` and then the chart container can flex. 

Now write everything.

**src/plugins/chart.tsx** — chart plugin with Card-like frame.

I'll create a shared `WidgetCard` component: a div (not uikit Card — Card has its own padding/views; a styled div with tokens is fine, or use uikit Card view="raised"). Use uikit `Card`? Card props: view, type, size, onClick... A simple styled div using tokens (`background: var(--g-color-base-float)`, border, radius token) is predictable. But skill prefers real components. uikit `Card` with `view="raised"`? Hmm, Card default view is 'normal' with border. Let me use Card with view="raised" for widgets. Card supports className/style. That's a real uikit component and gives shadow/border theme-aware. Good.

Widget frame layout:
```tsx
<Card view="raised" className="widget-card">
  <Text variant="subheader-1" className="widget-card__title">{title}</Text>
  <div className="widget-card__body"><Chart data={...}/></div>
</Card>
```
CSS: `.widget-card { height: 100%; display:flex; flex-direction:column; padding: var(--g-spacing-4) } .widget-card__body { flex:1; min-height:0 }`.

**src/plugins/metric.tsx** — metric/stat widget: label (caption), big value (display-2), delta with color (success up / danger down).

**src/plugins/templates.ts** — widget templates used both for initial config and for ActionPanel drops:

```ts
type WidgetKind = 'title' | 'text' | 'chart-line' | 'chart-bar' | 'chart-pie' | 'metric';
```
Each template: `{type, data, defaultLayout?}`. On drop, `dragProps.extra` carries the template id; or dragProps.type must be the plugin type, and extra carries which template. ItemDragProps: `{type, layout?, extra?}`. 

I'll use `extra: {templateId: 'chart-line'}`.

**onDrop**:
```ts
const handleDrop = (dropProps: ItemDropProps) => {
  const templateId = dropProps.dragProps.extra?.templateId;
  const template = WIDGET_TEMPLATES[templateId];
  const newConfig = DashKit.setItem({
    item: {
      type: template.type,
      namespace: 'default',
      data: template.data(),
      layout: dropProps.itemLayout, // has i? itemLayout includes i: TEMPORARY? Let me handle: layout expects Omit<ConfigLayout,'i'>
    },
    config,
    options: {updateLayout: dropProps.newLayout},
  });
  setConfig(newConfig);
  dropProps.commit();
};
```
Wait — `dropProps.itemLayout` is a ConfigLayout with `i` maybe as temporary id. AddConfigItem.layout is `Omit<ConfigLayout, 'i'>`. If itemLayout has i: '__dropping-elem__', passing it along with extra `i` prop to layout would be a type error (object literal with unknown prop). I'll strip: `const {i: _i, ...layout} = dropProps.itemLayout`. And updateLayout: dropProps.newLayout — but newLayout may include the temporary placeholder item with `i: '__dropping-elem__'`. In DataLens examples they do: they compute newLayout replacing the temp id... Let me check how storybook example handles onDrop. Search in dashkit repo? Not available offline. Hmm.

Let me think about what DashKit does internally on drop: The DashKit component receives onDrop; the parent adds the item and commits. If I pass updateLayout: newLayout that contains `__dropping-elem__` while my new item gets its own generated id, the layout would contain a stale entry. setItem with item.layout given inserts layout for new item; options.updateLayout replaces existing items' layouts — the temp entry would remain with no matching item → probably filtered or breaks.

Safer approach used in DashKit storybook (from memory of the actual repo): 

```ts
const onDrop = (dropProps: ItemDropProps) => {
  const {commit, dragProps, itemLayout, newLayout} = dropProps;
  const newConfig = DashKit.setItem({
    item: {...},
    config,
    options: {updateLayout: newLayout.filter(({i}) => i !== TEMPORARY_ITEM_ID)},
  });
  setConfig(newConfig);
  commit();
};
```
Hmm, actually I recall the storybook example:
```ts
onDrop={(dropProps) => {
  const newConfig = DashKit.setItem({
    item: {type: dragProps.type, namespace: 'default', data: {...}, layout: dropProps.itemLayout},
    config,
    options: {updateLayout: dropProps.newLayout},
  });
  setConfig(newConfig);
  dropProps.commit();
}}
```
Let me check how setItem handles updateLayout containing the temp id. Search UpdateManager setItem logic.

