**setup.ts**:
```ts
import {Lang, configure} from '@gravity-ui/uikit';
import {DashKit, pluginText, pluginTitle} from '@gravity-ui/dashkit';
import {chartPlugin} from './plugins/chart';
import {metricPlugin} from './plugins/metric';

let initialized = false;

export function setupDashboard() {
  if (initialized) return;
  initialized = true;
  configure({lang: Lang.Ru});
  DashKit.setSettings({
    gridLayout: {margin: [8, 8]},
  });
  DashKit.registerPlugins(pluginTitle, pluginText, chartPlugin, metricPlugin);
}
```
Wait — `DashKit.registerPlugins` with React StrictMode: module-level registerPlugins could double-run? It's called once per module evaluation; StrictMode double-renders components but module code runs once. But guard anyway since App render may call setupDashboard multiple times. Note registerPlugins throws if type already registered — the guard prevents that.

Actually cleaner: run setup at module top-level of setup.ts and import for side effect in App. With HMR it might re-run... guard handles re-run anyway. I'll export `setupDashboard()` and call it in module scope of `setup.ts` itself, then import './setup' in App. Simple and HMR-safe via the guard. But careful: registerPlugins throws on duplicate; guard handles it.

**gridLayout margin [8,8]** — plus default rowHeight 18, cols 36, containerPadding [0,0].

**chart.tsx**:
```tsx
import React from 'react';
import {Chart} from '@gravity-ui/charts';
import type {ChartData} from '@gravity-ui/charts';
import type {Plugin, PluginWidgetProps} from '@gravity-ui/dashkit';
import {WidgetFrame} from './WidgetFrame';

export interface ChartWidgetData {
  title: string;
  chart: ChartData;
}

function ChartWidget(props: PluginWidgetProps) {
  const {title, chart} = props.data as unknown as ChartWidgetData;
  return (
    <WidgetFrame title={title}>
      <Chart data={chart} lang="ru" />
    </WidgetFrame>
  );
}

export const chartPlugin: Plugin = {
  type: 'chart',
  defaultLayout: {w: 12, h: 14, minW: 8, minH: 8},
  renderer(props, forwardedRef) {
    return <ChartWidget {...props} ref={forwardedRef} />;
  },
};
```
Hmm — ref on function component errors. Use React.forwardRef for ChartWidget and pass ref to WidgetFrame root div. PluginRef = object | null; forwardedRef is `((instance: PluginRef) => void) | undefined`. A callback ref receiving DOM element works (object). With forwardRef<HTMLDivElement, PluginWidgetProps>, ref types: forwardedRef type is `(instance: PluginRef) => void` — passing to forwardRef div ref expects `Ref<HTMLDivElement>`. Type mismatch? `(instance: PluginRef) => void` where PluginRef = object | null — HTMLDivElement extends object so the callback accepting object|null is assignable to Ref<HTMLDivElement>? A callback `(o: object|null) => void` is assignable to `(el: HTMLDivElement|null) => void` — yes (parameter bivariance/contravariance: HTMLDivElement is subtype of object, so a function accepting object accepts HTMLDivElement). Fine.

To keep it simpler, I can ignore the ref: `renderer: (props) => <ChartWidget {...props} />`. The ref is used by DashKit for measuring (adjustWidgetLayout). Built-in plugins forward it. I'll forward it to the root div for correctness — small effort.

