Good — 'l' and 'm' are valid title sizes. Now App.tsx and styles.css.

App.tsx plan:

```tsx
import React from 'react';
import './setup';

import {ActionPanel, DashKit, DashKitDnDWrapper} from '@gravity-ui/dashkit';
import type {Config, ItemDropProps, ItemsStateAndParams} from '@gravity-ui/dashkit';
import {ArrowRotateLeft, ChartColumn, ChartLine, ChartPie, Hashtag, Heading, Moon, Sun, TextAlignLeft} from '@gravity-ui/icons';
import {Button, Flex, Icon, Switch, Text, ThemeProvider, Tooltip} from '@gravity-ui/uikit';

import {WIDGET_TEMPLATES, WidgetTemplateId, createInitialConfig} from './templates';
import './styles.css';
```

Check: are `Config`, `ItemDropProps`, `ItemsStateAndParams` exported from dashkit root? Root index: `export * from "./shared/types/index.js"` — shared/types/index.d.ts re-exports config.js etc. ItemDropProps — where defined? In shared (used by DashKit.d.ts import from "../../shared/index.js"). shared/index.d.ts probably re-exports types. Fine — the README example used `DashKitProps['config']`. To be safe use `DashKitProps` from root and derive types? Both are exported almost surely. I'll import type {Config, ItemDropProps, ItemsStateAndParams, MenuItem} and fall back if typecheck fails.

ActionPanel items type: use `React.ComponentProps<typeof ActionPanel>['items']`.

localStorage persistence:
```ts
const STORAGE_KEY = 'gravity-dashboard:v1';
function loadConfig(): Config {
    try {
        const raw = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
        if (raw) { const parsed = JSON.parse(raw); if (parsed && Array.isArray(parsed.items) && Array.isArray(parsed.layout)) return parsed as Config; }
    } catch {}
    return createInitialConfig();
}
```
useEffect persist on config change. Reset: setConfig(createInitialConfig()).

Wait: saved config contains chart data — serializable, fine.

Layout of App:

```tsx
export function App() {
    const [config, setConfig] = React.useState<Config>(loadConfig);
    const [itemsStateAndParams, setItemsStateAndParams] = React.useState<ItemsStateAndParams>({});
    const [editMode, setEditMode] = React.useState(false);
    const [theme, setTheme] = React.useState<'light'|'dark'>('light');

    React.useEffect(() => {
        try { localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(config)); } catch {}
    }, [config]);

    const handleChange = React.useCallback(({config: nextConfig, itemsStateAndParams: nextState}: {config: Config; itemsStateAndParams: ItemsStateAndParams}) => {
        setConfig(nextConfig);
        setItemsStateAndParams(nextState);
    }, []);

    const handleDrop = React.useCallback((dropProps: ItemDropProps) => {
        const templateId = dropProps.dragProps.extra?.templateId as WidgetTemplateId | undefined;
        const template = templateId ? WIDGET_TEMPLATES[templateId] : undefined;
        if (template) {
            setConfig((current) =>
                DashKit.setItem({
                    item: {
                        type: template.type,
                        namespace: 'default',
                        data: template.createData(),
                        layout: dropProps.itemLayout,
                    },
                    config: current,
                    options: {updateLayout: dropProps.newLayout},
                }),
            );
        }
        dropProps.commit();
    }, []);
    ...
```

Type: `layout: dropProps.itemLayout` — AddConfigItem.layout is `Omit<ConfigLayout, 'i'>`; ConfigLayout assignable structurally. OK.

`DashKit.setItem` — item requires `type`, `data`. AddConfigItem extends Omit<ConfigItem,'id'|'namespace'> → includes orderId?, defaults?, data, type. ✓

Note: setItem callback receives current config — but DashKit.setItem is a static on the class imported from dashkit — fine.

Header:
```tsx
<header className="app__header">
    <Flex justifyContent="space-between" alignItems="center" gap={4}>
        <Flex alignItems="center" gap={3} style={{minWidth: 0}}>
            <Text variant="header-1">Панель показателей</Text>
        </Flex>
        <Flex alignItems="center" gap={4} style={{flexShrink: 0}}>
            <Switch checked={editMode} onUpdate={setEditMode} size="l" content="Режим правки" />
            <Tooltip content="Сбросить раскладку">
                <Button view="outlined" onClick={handleReset}>
                    <Icon data={ArrowRotateLeft} size={16} />
                    Сбросить
                </Button>
            </Tooltip>
            <Tooltip content={theme === 'light' ? 'Тёмная тема' : 'Светлая тема'}>
                <Button view="flat" onClick={toggleTheme}>
                    <Icon data={theme === 'light' ? Moon : Sun} size={18} />
                </Button>
            </Tooltip>
        </Flex>
    </Flex>
</header>
```

Does Tooltip wrap Button fine — yes.

Switch `content` prop ✓.

ActionPanel items (memo):
```tsx
const actionPanelItems = React.useMemo<React.ComponentProps<typeof ActionPanel>['items']>(() => [
    {id: 'title', title: 'Заголовок', icon: <Icon data={Heading} size={16} />, dragProps: {type: 'title', layout: WIDGET_TEMPLATES.title.dragLayout, extra: {templateId: 'title'}}},
    ...
], []);
```

ItemDragProps.layout: `{w?: number; h?: number}` ✓ matches dragLayout.

Main:
```tsx
<main className="app__main">
    <DashKitDnDWrapper>
        <DashKit
            config={config}
            editMode={editMode}
            itemsStateAndParams={itemsStateAndParams}
            onChange={handleChange}
            onDrop={handleDrop}
        />
        {editMode && <ActionPanel items={actionPanelItems} />}
    </DashKitDnDWrapper>
</main>
```

Wait — DashKitDnDWrapper wraps both? README example:
```jsx
<DashKitDnDWrapper>
  <DashKit editMode config onChange onDrop />
  <ActionPanel items={overlayMenuItems} />
</DashKitDnDWrapper>
```
✓.

DashKit required props: config, editMode required; others have defaults (defaultGlobalParams etc. in defaultProps). onChange/onDrop have defaults (noop?) — but I pass them anyway. itemsStateAndParams default `{}`.

styles.css:
```css
.app-root {
    min-height: 100vh;
    background: var(--g-color-base-background);
    color: var(--g-color-text-primary);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
}

.app__header {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding: var(--g-spacing-4) var(--g-spacing-6);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--g-color-line-generic);
    background: var(--g-color-base-background);
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 10;  // sticky header
}

.app__main {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    padding: var(--g-spacing-4) var(--g-spacing-6) var(--g-spacing-10);
}

.widget-frame {
    height: 100%;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding: var(--g-spacing-4) var(--g-spacing-5);
    overflow: hidden;
}

.widget-frame__header { flex-shrink: 0; margin-bottom: var(--g-spacing-2); }
.widget-frame__body { flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
.widget-frame__body > *? Chart fills parent — Chart root presumably width/height 100% of its container. The container (.widget-frame__body) has computed height from flex. Chart "fills its parent" — parent needs a height; flex-basis resolved height works.

.metric-widget { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--g-spacing-2); justify-content: center; height: 100%; }
.metric-widget__delta { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--g-spacing-1); }
.metric-widget__delta-icon_up { color: var(--g-color-text-positive); }
.metric-widget__delta-icon_down { color: var(--g-color-text-danger); }
```

The app structure: ThemeProvider scoped div gets className app-root → it becomes flex column with min-height 100vh. Children: header + main directly inside? I planned `.app` wrapper — skip extra wrapper; put className on sections directly under ThemeProvider:

```tsx
<ThemeProvider theme={theme} rootClassName="app-root">
    <header className="app__header">…</header>
    <main className="app__main">…</main>
</ThemeProvider>
```

Wait — does rootClassName apply in non-scoped mode? Nested → scoped ✓.

Hmm — one concern: `.g-root` class on scoped div sets background? uikit `.g-root` sets background-color: var(--g-color-base-background)? In uikit, `g-root` class applies base background/color only on body-level root? styles.css `.g-root` typically sets background and color. I set them explicitly anyway. ✓

Also DashKit edit mode overlay dims items (opacity 0.5) and shows overlay — that's normal dashkit behavior.

The ActionPanel is positioned fixed bottom center by its CSS (dashkit default). OK.

Also in edit mode the widget content has pointer-events none (dashkit overlay handles interactions), in view mode charts are interactive. 

One more detail: `DashKit` in view mode — items not draggable (editMode false). ✓ "пользователь может свободно располагать по сетке" — achieved via edit mode: drag, resize, delete, copy; plus adding from action panel.

Default editMode: start in view mode or edit mode? Start with editMode=false for a clean first impression; user toggles "Режим правки". Hmm, but then dragging isn't immediately discoverable. The switch is prominent in the header. I'll default editMode=false, and mention in the summary.

Now — will `<Chart>` render inside RGL GridItem that uses CSS transforms? Chart measures with ResizeObserver on the wrapper div. Fine.

One risk: `@gravity-ui/charts` imports `d3` ESM — vite handles it.

Another risk: dashkit ESM build imports `react-grid-layout` css? RGL requires its own CSS ('react-grid-layout/css/styles.css')! DashKit's build — does it import RGL css? Let me check: grep "react-grid-layout/css" in dashkit esm.

